Miguel uses 2 pads of paper per week, with each pad containing 30 sheets. 

First, calculate the sheets used per week:
\[
2 \text{ pads/week} \times 30 \text{ sheets/pad} = 60 \text{ sheets/week}
\]

Next, determine the sheets used in a month (assuming 4 weeks):
\[
60 \text{ sheets/week} \times 4 \text{ weeks/month} = 240 \text{ sheets/month}
\]

\boxed{240}

Regina wrote 9 novels last year, which is 3/4 of the total novels she has written this year. To find the total, divide 9 by 3/4:  
9 ÷ (3/4) = 9 × (4/3) = 12.  

\boxed{12}

Janet initially has 22 green pens and 10 yellow pens, totaling 32 pens. She buys 6 bags of blue pens (6 × 9 = 54 pens) and 2 bags of red pens (2 × 6 = 12 pens). Adding these to her initial total:  
32 + 54 + 12 = 98.

\boxed{98}

To determine how many days Alfie needs to fly half the Earth's circumference, first calculate half the circumference:  
\[
\frac{40,000 \text{ km}}{2} = 20,000 \text{ km}
\]  
Next, divide the total distance by his daily flight:  
\[
\frac{20,000 \text{ km}}{400 \text{ km/day}} = 50 \text{ days}
\]  

\boxed{50}

